      },
      "rev_n_6_ca48c2_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_ca48c2_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_ca48c2",
        "thought_text": "Consider using a more efficient data structure for counting and comparison, such as a defaultdict or array instead of Counter, to potentially improve performance.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8637067825375367273",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794537251.342718
      },
      "rev_n_4_e7e5bb_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_e7e5bb_1",
        "node_id": "n_4_e7e5bb",
        "thought_text": "The loop condition should be `range(1, n - m * p + 2)` to include all possible starting positions, including the last valid one. This correction ensures that the function checks all potential starting points for sequence b within sequence a, addressing the oversight in the previous implementation.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8637067825375367273",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_4_e7e5bb_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_0347b853d8",
        "created_at": 1794537276.4732149
      },
      "rev_n_5_38888c_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_38888c_1",
        "node_id": "n_5_38888c",
        "thought_text": "The error in the original code was due to an incorrect loop condition. The condition `n - m * p + 2` should be `n - m * p + 1` to include the last valid starting position. This oversight caused the function to miss the last possible valid q in some cases. To optimize the counting process, we can use a sliding window approach to avoid redundant calculations. This approach will maintain a window of size m and slide it across the sequence a, updating the counts efficiently as we go.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8637067825375367273",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_5_38888c_0",
